=========================================================
        KUBERNETES ADVANCED TROUBLESHOOTING NOTES
=========================================================

These scenarios are commonly encountered in production and
are frequently asked in DevOps/Kubernetes interviews.

=========================================================
1. EVENTS (First place to look)
=========================================================

What are Events?
Events are Kubernetes messages that explain what happened
to a resource.

Common Errors:
- FailedScheduling
- FailedMount
- FailedAttachVolume
- FailedCreatePodSandBox
- Back-off restarting container
- ErrImagePull
- ImagePullBackOff

Commands:
kubectl get events
kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
kubectl describe pod <pod>

Example:
Pod Pending -> Check events -> "0/3 nodes available:
Insufficient memory."


=========================================================
2. SERVICE AND ENDPOINT ISSUES
=========================================================

Service sends traffic to Endpoints.

Service
   |
   V
Endpoints
   |
   V
Pods

Common Issues:
- Service selector mismatch.
- Pods not Ready.
- Labels missing.
- No endpoints available.

Commands:
kubectl get svc
kubectl describe svc
kubectl get endpoints

Example:
Service selector:
app=nginx

Pod label:
app=web

Result:
No endpoints created.


=========================================================
3. DNS PROBLEMS
=========================================================

Pods communicate using DNS.

Example:
mysql.default.svc.cluster.local

Common Issues:
- Service name cannot resolve.
- CoreDNS failure.
- Wrong service name.

Commands:
kubectl exec -it pod -- nslookup service
kubectl exec -it pod -- ping service
kubectl get pods -n kube-system

Check CoreDNS:
kubectl get pods -n kube-system


=========================================================
4. NETWORK POLICY
=========================================================

NetworkPolicy controls pod-to-pod communication.

Common Issues:
- Traffic blocked.
- Database inaccessible.
- Microservices cannot communicate.

Commands:
kubectl get networkpolicy
kubectl describe networkpolicy

Example:
Frontend pod cannot access backend pod because
NetworkPolicy denies traffic.


=========================================================
5. STORAGE ISSUES
=========================================================

Storage Components:

Application
    |
    V
PVC
    |
    V
PV
    |
    V
StorageClass

Common Issues:
- PVC Pending.
- PV unavailable.
- Wrong StorageClass.
- Volume attachment failure.
- CSI driver issues.

Commands:
kubectl get pvc
kubectl get pv
kubectl describe pvc
kubectl describe pod


=========================================================
6. DEPLOYMENT ISSUES
=========================================================

Deployment manages ReplicaSets and Pods.

Common Issues:
- Rollout stuck.
- Wrong image.
- Failed deployment.
- Pod startup failures.

Commands:
kubectl get deployment
kubectl describe deployment
kubectl rollout status deployment/app
kubectl rollout history deployment/app

Rollback:
kubectl rollout undo deployment/app


=========================================================
7. REPLICASET ISSUES
=========================================================

ReplicaSet ensures desired number of pods.

Common Issues:
- Pod creation failure.
- Resource shortage.
- Scheduling failure.

Commands:
kubectl get rs
kubectl describe rs


=========================================================
8. CRASHLOOPBACKOFF
=========================================================

Container starts and crashes repeatedly.

Common Causes:
- Application bug.
- Wrong environment variables.
- Missing Secret.
- Database unavailable.
- OOMKilled.
- Health check failure.

Commands:
kubectl logs pod
kubectl logs pod --previous
kubectl describe pod

Interview Tip:
Always check previous logs.


=========================================================
9. RESOURCE LIMITS
=========================================================

Resources:
- CPU
- Memory

Common Issues:
- CPU throttling.
- Memory exhaustion.
- OOMKilled.

Commands:
kubectl top pod
kubectl top node
kubectl describe pod

Example:
Exit Code 137
Reason:
OOMKilled.


=========================================================
10. NAMESPACE ISSUES
=========================================================

Resource exists but in another namespace.

Commands:
kubectl get all -n namespace
kubectl config current-context

Example:
Service in namespace A
Pod in namespace B


=========================================================
11. RBAC ISSUES
=========================================================

RBAC controls permissions.

Components:
- Role
- ClusterRole
- RoleBinding
- ClusterRoleBinding

Common Error:
Forbidden

Commands:
kubectl auth can-i
kubectl get roles
kubectl get rolebindings
kubectl get clusterroles

Example:
User cannot create deployments.


=========================================================
12. INGRESS ISSUES
=========================================================

Ingress routes external traffic.

Internet
   |
   V
Ingress
   |
   V
Service
   |
   V
Pods

Common Issues:
- Controller not running.
- Wrong ingress class.
- Wrong backend service.
- TLS issue.
- DNS issue.

Commands:
kubectl get ingress
kubectl describe ingress


=========================================================
13. TLS/CERTIFICATE ISSUES
=========================================================

Common Issues:
- Certificate expired.
- Wrong certificate.
- TLS handshake failure.
- Secret missing.

Commands:
kubectl get secrets
kubectl describe ingress


=========================================================
14. NODE ISSUES
=========================================================

Common Problems:
- NotReady
- MemoryPressure
- DiskPressure
- PIDPressure
- NetworkUnavailable

Commands:
kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node

Example:
Node under DiskPressure.
Pods cannot schedule.


=========================================================
15. EVICTED PODS
=========================================================

Pod removed due to node resource shortage.

Reasons:
- Low memory.
- Low disk.

Commands:
kubectl describe pod

Example:
Status:
Evicted


=========================================================
16. IMAGE PULL SECRET ISSUES
=========================================================

Private registry authentication failure.

Common Errors:
ErrImagePull
ImagePullBackOff

Commands:
kubectl get secrets
kubectl describe pod


=========================================================
17. CNI NETWORK ISSUES
=========================================================

CNI provides pod networking.

Common Problems:
- Pod IP not assigned.
- Pods cannot communicate.
- Cross-node communication failure.

Commands:
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
kubectl describe pod


=========================================================
18. CONFIGMAP AND SECRET ISSUES
=========================================================

Common Issues:
- Missing ConfigMap.
- Missing Secret.
- Wrong key.
- Invalid values.

Commands:
kubectl get configmap
kubectl get secrets
kubectl describe pod


=========================================================
19. INIT CONTAINER FAILURES
=========================================================

Init containers must complete before app starts.

Common Issues:
- Database unavailable.
- Script failure.
- Volume mount issue.

Commands:
kubectl logs pod -c init-container
kubectl describe pod


=========================================================
20. APPLICATION DEPENDENCY ISSUES
=========================================================

Application may depend on:
- Database
- Redis
- Kafka
- External APIs

Common Problems:
- Dependency unavailable.
- DNS failure.
- Authentication failure.

Check:
Application logs
Connectivity
Service endpoints


